<!-- Question 1 -->

<div class="section">
<h2>1. HTTP Response Analysis</h2>

<pre>
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Date: Mon, 01 Apr 2026 10:00:00 GMT
Content-Type: text/html
Content-Length: -150
Connection: keep-alive
</pre>

<p><b>1. Identify any incorrect or invalid fields in the header.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
Content-Length is invalid because it contains a negative value.
</div>

<p><b>2. Analyze the issue with Content-Length and explain its impact.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
Content-Length specifies the size of the response body in bytes. A negative value may cause browsers to reject or improperly display the webpage.
</div>

<p><b>3. Determine whether the status code matches the response correctness.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
The status code 200 OK indicates success, but the invalid Content-Length field makes the response incorrect.
</div>

<p><b>4. Suggest corrections for the identified errors.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
Replace Content-Length with a positive value such as 150.
</div>
</div>



<!-- Question 2 -->

<div class="section">
<h2>2. DOM Structure Analysis</h2>

<pre>
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Test Page&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Hello World
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</pre>

<p><b>1. Identify missing or improperly closed elements.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
The paragraph tag (&lt;p&gt;) is missing its closing tag (&lt;/p&gt;).
</div>

<p><b>2. Analyze how the browser will interpret this structure.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
Modern browsers automatically close missing tags and display the content correctly.
</div>

<p><b>3. Determine the impact on rendering and layout.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
The webpage may render properly, but the HTML structure is invalid and may cause issues in complex webpages.
</div>

<p><b>4. Suggest corrections to make the DOM valid.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
Add the missing &lt;/p&gt; tag before the closing &lt;/div&gt; tag.
</div>
</div>



<!-- Question 3 -->

<div class="section">
<h2>3. GET vs POST in Login System</h2>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Method</th>
<th>URL Example</th>
<th>Data Visibility</th>
<th>Security Level</th>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>GET</td>
<td>/login?user=admin&pass=1234</td>
<td>Visible in URL</td>
<td>Low</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>POST</td>
<td>/login</td>
<td>Hidden in Request Body</td>
<td>Moderate</td>
</tr>
</table>

<br>

<p><b>1. Interpret the differences in data transmission between GET and POST.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
GET sends data through the URL, whereas POST sends data through the request body.
</div>

<p><b>2. Analyze which method is more suitable for login and why.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
POST is more suitable because login credentials are not exposed in the URL.
</div>

<p><b>3. Identify security risks in using GET for authentication.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
Passwords may be stored in browser history, bookmarks, and server logs.
</div>

<p><b>4. Suggest best practices for secure login implementation.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
Use POST requests, HTTPS, secure password storage, and proper session management.
</div>
</div>



<!-- Question 4 -->

<div class="section">
<h2>4. HTML Code Inspection</h2>

<pre>
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
&lt;title&gt;Sample&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Welcome
&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph
&lt;img src="image.jpg"&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</pre>

<p><b>1. Identify missing closing tags.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
The closing tags &lt;/h1&gt; and &lt;/p&gt; are missing.
</div>

<p><b>2. Analyze issues with the img tag.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
The image tag should include an alt attribute for accessibility and the image file should exist in the correct location.
</div>

<p><b>3. Determine how browsers will handle these errors.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
Browsers automatically correct many minor HTML errors, but the code is not standards compliant.
</div>

<p><b>4. Suggest a corrected version of the code.</b></p>

<pre>
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
&lt;title&gt;Sample&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;Welcome&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;img src="image.jpg" alt="Sample Image"&gt;

&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</pre>

</div>



<!-- Question 5 -->

<div class="section">
<h2>5. Browser-Server Communication Flow</h2>

<ol>
<li>User enters a URL in the browser.</li>
<li>DNS lookup occurs.</li>
<li>HTTP request is sent.</li>
<li>Server processes the request.</li>
<li>Response is returned.</li>
<li>Browser renders the webpage.</li>
</ol>

<p><b>1. Interpret each step in the communication flow.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
The browser locates the web server, sends the request, receives the response, and displays the webpage.
</div>

<p><b>2. Identify where delays can occur.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
Delays may occur during DNS lookup, network communication, server processing, and browser rendering.
</div>

<p><b>3. Analyze the role of DNS in the process.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
DNS converts the domain name into an IP address so that the browser can communicate with the correct server.
</div>

<p><b>4. Suggest optimizations to improve performance.</b></p>
<div class="answer">
Use DNS caching, browser caching, CDNs, compressed resources, and optimized server responses.
</div>
